// Output-lift suite. One JSON object per line; `//` comments and blank lines are ignored.
//
// Routing (`routing.jsonl`) tests WHICH skill fires. This suite tests whether the skill that
// fired produced a BETTER answer than the same model with no skill at all. Every case runs
// twice, once with the skill body loaded and once bare, and both runs are scored against the
// same criteria. The number that matters is the gap between them, not the absolute pass rate.
//
// Schema, per line:
//   case        directory-safe identifier, unique across the suite
//   skill       frontmatter `name:` of the skill under test (not the directory)
//   prompt      what a teammate would actually type
//   checks      OPTIONAL deterministic gates, scored offline with no model call:
//                 required            regexes that must each match the response
//                 forbidden           regexes that must not match
//                 max_words           ceiling on prose words (fenced code excluded)
//                 max_sentence_words  ceiling on the longest single sentence
//                 scope               "response" (default) or "unquoted". "unquoted" strips
//                                     fenced code, backticks and quoted spans first, so a
//                                     check measures what the skill WROTE, not what it
//                                     CITED. Required for any de-slopping skill: it names
//                                     the phrases it removed, and a naive forbidden-word
//                                     scan punishes it for doing so. "deliverable" scores
//                                     only a ```deliverable fenced block; use it when the
//                                     skill's analysis legitimately names what the check
//                                     forbids, and ask for the block in the prompt.
//   assertions  prose criteria a grader model scores PASS/FAIL with one line of evidence
//   why         the wrong answer this case rules out. Required: a failing case with no `why`
//               is undiagnosable six months later.
//
// Only seed cases for skills whose deliverable is TEXT RETURNED IN THE REPLY. A skill that
// writes to monday, posts to Slack, or stops at a human gate cannot be scored from a headless
// one-shot, and a case that pretends otherwise measures the harness rather than the skill.
// See evals/README.md.


{"case": "ste-status-update-strips-slop", "skill": "ste", "prompt": "Rewrite this status update so it is clear and direct: 'Hey team! Great question on where we landed. So basically, in order to facilitate the migration we've been leveraging the new sync layer, and at this point in time we're seeing some pretty robust improvements across the board. It's not just faster, it's fundamentally transformative. We processed approximately 41,000 records and the error rate went from 3.2% to 0.4%. Moving forward we'll circle back on the remaining edge cases.' Put the rewrite itself in a fenced block tagged deliverable, then explain what you changed.", "checks": {"forbidden": ["(?i)\\bleverag", "(?i)\\butiliz", "(?i)\\bfacilitat", "(?i)in order to", "(?i)great question", "(?i)at this point in time", "(?i)circle back", "(?i)moving forward", "(?i)across the board", "(?i)it'?s not just .{1,40}, it'?s", "\u2014"], "max_words": 120, "max_sentence_words": 25, "scope": "deliverable"}, "assertions": ["The first sentence states the outcome, not context or throat-clearing", "Every figure from the input survives with its exact number: 41,000 records, 3.2%, 0.4%", "Sentences are active voice and name who does the action", "No hype adjective survives anywhere in the rewrite"], "why": "guards the whole point of ste: baseline rewrites this into smoother prose rather than stripping it to plain controlled sentences, and it tends to round or drop the figures on the way"}

{"case": "ste-mixed-request-splits-the-pipeline", "skill": "ste", "prompt": "Analyze last week's paid search numbers and then draft the LinkedIn post announcing the result.", "checks": {"required": ["(?i)linkedin"]}, "assertions": ["Applies STE plainness to the analysis half only", "States explicitly which half got STE and which half did not", "Names nik-voice then de-ai as the path for the LinkedIn post rather than writing it flat itself", "Does not produce a LinkedIn post that reads like a runbook"], "why": "ste's body carries one explicit boundary for mixed requests and it is the rule most likely to be lost: baseline applies a single register to both halves, usually flattening the social post into instructions"}

{"case": "de-ai-replaces-rather-than-deletes", "skill": "de-ai", "prompt": "Clean this up: 'It's important to note that our comprehensive approach to creator partnerships leverages a robust set of tools. Furthermore, it's worth mentioning that we delve into each partner's landscape before onboarding. This ensures we foster alignment and navigate the complexities of co-marketing. Needless to say, don't hesitate to reach out.' Put the cleaned version in a fenced block tagged deliverable, then explain what you changed.", "checks": {"forbidden": ["(?i)\\bleverag", "(?i)\\bdelv", "(?i)\\brobust\\b", "(?i)\\bcomprehensive\\b", "(?i)\\bfoster\\b", "(?i)\\bensures?\\b", "(?i)\\bfurthermore\\b", "(?i)\\bmoreover\\b", "(?i)it'?s important to note", "(?i)it'?s worth mentioning", "(?i)needless to say", "(?i)don'?t hesitate to", "(?i)the .{1,20} landscape", "\u2014"], "scope": "deliverable"}, "assertions": ["Each flagged phrase was swapped for a plainer equivalent rather than silently deleted", "The factual content is unchanged: creator partnerships, tooling, a pre-onboarding review, co-marketing, an invitation to reply", "The deliverable is no longer than the input it replaced", "Reads like a coworker typed it, not like a shorter version of the same corporate register"], "why": "de-ai's central instruction is never just delete, always replace. Baseline deletes the flagged phrases and leaves stumps, which scores clean on a forbidden-word check while reading worse than the original"}

{"case": "de-ai-density-triage-sends-it-back", "skill": "de-ai", "prompt": "De-slop this: 'In today's fast-paced digital landscape, it's crucial to leverage comprehensive solutions. Moreover, organizations must delve into robust frameworks that foster innovation. It's not just about technology, it's about transformation. Needless to say, this pivotal shift ensures stakeholders navigate complexity while driving meaningful outcomes at scale.'", "checks": {"required": ["(?i)nik-voice", "(?i)rewrit"]}, "assertions": ["Counts or names the density of tells before attempting any fix", "Declines to polish in place and says why", "Sends the draft back to nik-voice and names the register it should use", "Does not hand back a lightly-edited version of the same sentences"], "why": "the 10-tells-per-500-words triage rule is the one instruction telling the skill to REFUSE the obvious task. Baseline always polishes, which is exactly the failure the rule exists to stop"}

{"case": "critique-verdict-not-rewrite", "skill": "critique", "prompt": "Critique this LinkedIn draft before it ships: 'In today's fast-paced content landscape, authentic video truly stands out. We've helped creators leverage remote recording to boost engagement and drive results. The future of podcasting is here. What do you think?'", "checks": {"required": ["(?m)^VERDICT:\\s*(SHIP|REVISE)\\b", "(?i)point first", "(?i)specific", "(?i)non-?obvious", "(?i)length", "(?i)peer", "(?i)clean"]}, "assertions": ["Scores all six dimensions, including the ones that pass, not only the failures", "Every Flag carries a one-line reason and a specific fix", "Does not hand back a rewritten version of the draft", "Names at least one concrete slop tell present in the input rather than describing the problem in general terms"], "why": "critique is a gate that must not become an editor. Baseline reflexively rewrites the draft, which silently converts a check into a writing pass and removes the independent judgement the gate exists to provide"}

{"case": "minto-pyramid-bottom-up-status-to-top-down", "skill": "minto-pyramid", "prompt": "Nir asked 'where are we on the pricing page test?'. Restructure this reply so it works for him: 'Background: we launched the pricing page test on 2026-09-08 in Convert with two variants. Findings: traffic has been split 50/50 and about 9,800 visitors have seen it. Variant B (annual toggle default) shows 4.1% plan-page-to-checkout vs 3.4% for control. Convert says significance is at 91% and we set 95% as the bar. Mobile is flat, desktop carries the lift. Next steps: we think it will reach significance in about 6 more days at current traffic. Summary: overall B looks promising.' Put the rewrite in a fenced block tagged deliverable.", "checks": {"forbidden": ["(?im)^\\W*(background|findings|summary|overview|key takeaways)\\W*:?\\W*$", "(?i)\\bbackground:", "(?i)\\bfindings:", "(?i)\\bsummary:", "\u2014", "\u2013"], "max_words": 130, "scope": "deliverable"}, "assertions": ["The first sentence answers Nir's question (where the test stands and what it means) rather than giving launch context", "Every figure survives exactly: 50/50 traffic split, 9,800 visitors, 4.1%, 3.4%, 91%, 95%, about 6 days", "Supporting points are the same kind of idea and in a nameable order", "No closing line restates the opening; it ends on a next step, a date, or nothing", "Makes clear the result is not yet significant, so the top line does not overclaim a win"], "why": "guards the core pyramid move: baseline tidies the prose but keeps the Background/Findings/Summary order, so the answer stays buried at the bottom, and it tends to lead with 'B is winning' past the unmet significance bar"}
